Minkar: stage the kernel and move it after ExitBootServices

SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
Copyright (c) 2026 The Minkar contributors
Part of Minkar.

On a PC (Gigabyte H270N-WIFI) the loader found the kernel, printed its
header and the machine reset. The loader wrote to physical memory it had
never asked the firmware for: the kernelcache was read to a fixed 80 MB
address, the kernel's segments were copied from 1 MB up while boot
services were running, and the memory map was kept at 0x10000. The
kernel's range is not free under QEMU either (ACPI NVS at 8 MB,
BootServicesData from 9 MB); there it happened not to matter.

- The kernel is linked to run at 1 MB. Its segments, the device tree, the
  boot args and the EFI tables are laid out in a staging buffer allocated
  from the firmware, and moved to 1 MB after ExitBootServices(), when
  boot-services memory is free, as GRUB's relocator does for xnu.
- Before that, the loader checks the range: memory the firmware keeps
  (runtime services, ACPI tables, reserved, MMIO) stops the boot with the
  entries listed; ACPI NVS is reported and overwritten, as before. The
  range's free pages are taken so later allocations stay out of it, and
  the loader, its stack, the GDT and the page tables must lie outside it.
- The staging buffers are allocated from the firmware. The memory map is
  taken after the last allocation, right before ExitBootServices(), whose
  result is now checked; on a stale key the map is taken again. It goes
  after the EFI tables, in low memory, where the kernel can read it before
  it maps the rest of memory.
- The kernel's copy of the map has no EFI_MEMORY_RUNTIME attributes. With
  VirtualStart 0 (no SetVirtualAddressMap() call) efi_init() mapped every
  runtime region over the kernel's boot code. The old map at 0x10000 had
  been reused by the kernel before efi_init() read it, so nothing was ever
  mapped; that stays the behavior. The runtime services are still called
  at their physical addresses.
- After ExitBootServices() the loader runs on its own page tables (1:1,
  writable, executable, 2 MB pages), since a PC firmware's may keep its
  former memory read-only or no-execute, and sets EFER.NXE and CR0.WP
  before the jump: xnu's 32-bit entry set them, the 64-bit entry relied on
  the firmware. OVMF sets NXE; without it the kernel's first no-execute
  page table entry faults before it has a handler, and the machine resets.
- With loader-pause set, the loader prints the CPU state the firmware left
  (EFER, CR0, CR4), and after ExitBootServices() paints a white band at the
  top of the screen for each step it passes.
- The device tree has 16 pages (a PC has more configuration tables than
  QEMU), and its size is checked.
- "loader-pause" (integer, seconds) in org.minkar.boot.plist keeps the
  loader's output on the screen before the kernel starts.

